    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Released in 1974 on Curtom, Sweet Exorcist is Curtis Mayfield’s fifth solo album and a sharp example of his early-’70s blend of funk, soul, and socially conscious songwriting. Built around tight grooves, layered guitar lines, and his smooth falsetto, the record balances political urgency with intimate, reflective themes. Its best-known track, “Kung Fu,” became a strong R&B hit and gave the album a burst of popular momentum, while the title song and “Power to the People” show Mayfield at his most fluent in turning spiritual and social concerns into elegant, infectious music.\n\nThe album arrived during a highly productive period for Mayfield and has often been viewed as a transitional work: less monumental than Super Fly, but still rich in melodic craft and rhythmic detail. It reached No. 2 on Billboard’s R&B chart and No. 39 on the pop chart, and later critics have noted both its relaxed, understated feel and its strong songwriting. For listeners drawn to sophisticated 1970s funk and soul, Sweet Exorcist stands as one of Mayfield’s most distinctive Curtom-era releases.",
